data: {"type":"content_block_delta","index":1,"delta":{"type":"text_delta","text":"**A1 · Keep carts in Redis or Postgres?** is still waiting on your choice.\n\n**The short version:** The browser cookie is too small for a cart, so the cart has to live on the server. The question is which database holds it.\n\n**Example:** A shopper adds 30 items. Cookies are capped at about 4 KB, so the cart can't fit in the cookie. Instead the cookie carries only a session ID, and the server looks up the cart under that ID.\n\n**What each option leads to:**\n- **Redis (a1):** Reads and writes are very fast, and carts can expire automatically after a set time. The risk is durability. Unless Redis persistence is configured, a restart or eviction can silently empty carts. It also adds a new service if you don't already run Redis. This suits short-lived, high-traffic carts where losing one is acceptable.\n- **Postgres (a2, the main agent's pick):** Carts survive restarts. Checkout can join cart rows with products and prices, and write the order in one transaction. The cost is slightly slower cart reads and more load on the database. If Postgres is already in your stack, this adds nothing new to operate.\n\n**My recommendation:** Postgres (a2). Carts that vanish mid-shop or during checkout cost more than a few extra milliseconds per request. I'd switch to Redis only if you already run Redis, have very high cart traffic, and accept losing carts.\n\nI couldn't find cart or session code in the main checkout (its only commit is `init`), so this reasoning rests on the ask's text and general trade-offs, not your codebase. If you tell me whether the app already runs Postgres or Redis, I can narrow the choice. Which option do you want, a1 (Redis) or a2 (Postgres)?"}}

